"""Manually matched doctrine and two-categorical construction claims."""
unit(921,6,'Doctrines, constructions, and two categorical Yoneda',[81,82,83,84,85,86,87,88,90,91,92],'1–405','« grain de sel »; « pas clair ... U-catégorie »')

addclaim(86,921,6,[81],'40–45','Paraphrase: topoi with enough essential points are exactly presheaf topoi.','A Grothendieck topos has a conservative family of essential points iff it is equivalent to a presheaf topos. Essential points correspond to nonempty connected projectives; for PSh(C) their category is the Karoubi completion of C. An essential geometric morphism is characterized by its inverse image preserving all small limits, equivalently products.','stated without proof; universe warning follows','A point is essential iff its inverse image is represented; preservation of coproducts and epimorphisms makes the representing object connected nonempty projective. A generating family of such objects has trivial induced topology, yielding a presheaf presentation. Retracts of representables give exactly the essential points of PSh(C). This does not turn the phrase all exactness properties of Set into a complete independent axiom system.','SGA4I, ExposéIV Exercise7.6(a–g), printedpp414–417, is the exact published statement and explicitly credits the presheaf criterion to J.-E. Roos. The retyped text was checked here; the original facsimile was separately checked in the spaces work.',['SGA4I1972'],'A',event='targeted proof check')
addclaim(87,921,6,[82,83,84,85],'47–173','Paraphrase: rho(T)=Hom(T,tau), sigma(R)=Hom_Delta(R,-), and a represented type recovers its construction category.','With pseudonatural transformations and modifications, the two-Yoneda equivalence PsNat(Hom_Delta(R,-),T)≃T(R) gives rho(sigma R)≃R. Evaluation has direction T→sigma(rho T). Variance in R is contravariant, and the claimed category of constructions must lie in the chosen universe.','proof sketched; opposite diagram suppressed and reverse evaluation arrow canceled','Evaluate a transformation at id_R. Its inverse sends x∈T(R) to the transformation f↦T(f)(x); pseudofunctor coherence gives the inverse equivalences and the modification correspondence. Applied to the underlying-category pseudofunctor tau this recovers R. Evaluation sends a model to the functor evaluating each construction on that model, hence has the stated direction.','Vistoli2007§3.6.2pp62–63 gives the fibred two-Yoneda proof; Kelly§2.4 gives the enriched analogue. The pseudofunctor version is independently proved by the displayed identity evaluation, with all size assumptions exposed.',['VistoliDescent','Kelly1982'])
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(7)+':95–229');claims[-1]['scans'] += [99,100]
addclaim(88,921,6,[82,83,85,88],'80–88;130–173;295–314','Paraphrase: evaluation should universally approximate a type by a representable type, and all algebraic types should be representable.','If rho(T)=PsNat(T,tau) is a locally small object of the chosen doctrine and evaluation is doctrine-exact, currying gives PsNat(T,sigma R)≃Hom_Delta(R,rho T). Those assumptions do not follow from the definition of an arbitrary large type. Small bounded sketches and specific finitary exactness doctrines have established free constructions.','universal-reflection wording canceled at88; size and stabilization questions remain','Currying a transformation T→Hom_Delta(R,-) gives a functor R→PsNat(T,tau), and the exactness requirement is exactly what makes it a Delta-functor. This proves the conditional universal property, not that rho(T) is small or that T≃sigma rho(T). The manuscript’s large transfinite argument has no demonstrated stopping ordinal.','Kelly5.35 handles small bounded completion. Di Liberti–Osmond2025§6.3 and Theorem6.4.5/Corollary6.4.6 establish bipresentability for Lex and specified finitary exactness doctrines. They do not establish the unbounded arbitrary-Delta claim or remove its local-smallness question.',['Kelly1982','DiLibertiOsmond2025'],'C')
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(7)+':130–163;278–350');claims[-1]['scans'] += [99,102,103]
addclaim(89,921,6,[83,84],'121–166','Paraphrase: construct bicolimits through the total cofibred category, inverting its cartesian arrows, then force exactness.','For F:I→Cat, its pseudocolimit is the localization of the Grothendieck opfibration at its cocartesian arrows. A bicolimit in a doctrine can then be obtained by its free envelope and by inverting the required comparison arrows, provided those constructions exist with the stipulated size.','construction sketched; size doubt at136','A functor from the total category specifies the component functors and transition comparisons of a lax cocone. Sending cocartesian arrows to isomorphisms makes those comparisons invertible, which is precisely a pseudococone. The localization universal property therefore proves the pseudocolimit formula. Inverting limit-comparison maps subsequently enforces exactness only when the comparisons form admissible data.','Di Liberti–Osmond2025§1.2.16 gives the exact localization formula; Lemma6.3.2 proves the filtered lex case by computing finite limits at a common stage. The manuscript’s word cartesian is retained as a variance alarm, not silently replaced.',['DiLibertiOsmond2025','StacksLocalization'])
addclaim(90,921,6,[85,86,87],'175–273','Paraphrase: build theories by empty structure, new arrows, equality/invertibility axioms and products on a fixed basis.','Small theories can be presented by objects, arrows and relations, together with the prescribed operation comparisons. Equality of parallel arrows can be forced by inverting their equalizer inclusion when that equalizer exists. Independent products of represented types correspond to coproducts of classifiers; products over a common basis correspond to amalgamated coproducts.','rules stated and illustrated; equality-axiom question explicitly flagged','For f,g:X⇒Y, their equalizer e:E→X becomes invertible exactly when the preserved f,g become equal. Without equalizers, merely requiring f and g individually invertible does not impose f=g; one needs a relation quotient. Functors out of an ordinary or amalgamated classifier coproduct encode exactly independent or basis-compatible model pairs. A small category is presented using its complete small arrow set and composition relations.','Kelly5.35 gives the completion component; Stacks04VB gives universal inversion; Di Liberti–Osmond6.3–6.4 gives the finitary doctrine setting. These match the small presentation mechanism and leave the author’s large-family caveat intact.',['Kelly1982','StacksLocalization','DiLibertiOsmond2025'])
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(7)+':178–315');claims[-1]['scans'] += [100,101,102]
addclaim(91,921,6,[88],'295–314','Paraphrase: a large classifier with a small faithful basis should be assembled from small stages and eventually stabilize.','A small classifier has a small presentation. For a large locally small classifier, determination of transformations on a small basis alone does not prove operation generation or a uniform small stopping bound. The particular transfinite stabilization proposed on the sheet remains unproved at its stated level of generality.','open steps: full images, full faithfulness, conservativity and universe bound','C101 gives finite counterexamples to the proposed implication from a faithful basis to operation generation. Even after strengthening to generation, an unbounded family of shapes can evade a fixed regular closure ordinal. The small bounded theorem C082 supplies a valid replacement with an explicit bound, but cannot justify the unrestricted argument.','Searches through Kelly5.35 and modern doctrine bipresentability (Di Liberti–Osmond6.3–6.4) found the bounded/small results, not a theorem with these unrestricted quantifiers. This is a delimited residual formulation, not an assertion that no broader theorem exists.',['Kelly1982','DiLibertiOsmond2025'],'C')
claims[-1]['lines'].append(source_file(7)+':309–350');claims[-1]['scans'] += [102,103]
addclaim(92,921,6,[88],'300–314','Paraphrase: a conservative exact functor is faithful; fully faithful is struck out.','A conservative functor preserving equalizers is faithful. It need not be full.','stronger fullness wording canceled; faithful statement retained','If Ff=Fg, the image of the equalizer of f,g is an isomorphism, so conservativity makes the original equalizer an isomorphism and f=g. The forgetful functor Groups→Set preserves finite limits and reflects isomorphisms but is not full: many underlying-set maps do not preserve multiplication.','Riehl’s adjunction/limit framework supports the exact interpretation; the two-line proof and explicit counterexample are independently checked. This validates the retained claim while preserving the canceled strengthening.',['RiehlContextOnline'])
addclaim(93,921,6,[90],'316–338','Paraphrase: finite-limit models are generated by finitely presentable objects, whose envelope is stable under finite projective limits.','A small finite-limit sketch has a locally finitely presentable model category. Its finitely presentable objects are closed under finite colimits and retracts; they need not be closed under finite limits. In the manuscript setting with actual designated limits, the representable models generate the finite-colimit closure.','main model theorem sketched; finite-projective-limit closure false as written','Take R=k⊕V with V a countably infinite-dimensional square-zero ideal. Multiplication by a nonzero x∈V is an endomorphism R→R between finitely presented modules; its kernel is V, which is not finitely generated because R acts on V through k. Thus the kernel is not finitely presentable. Finite colimits and retracts of finitely presentable objects are finitely presentable by the Hom/filtered-colimit criterion.','Lack–Tendas Theorem4.4 and the model results5.12–5.14 give the sketch theorem; Lack–Power8 gives its finite-limit completion formulation. The noncoherent-ring example independently refutes the wrong-variance closure at line337.',['LackTendas2023','LackPower2009'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-093')
addclaim(94,921,6,[91,92],'341–405','Paraphrase: the multiple dictionary for arbitrary lambda identifies models, Ind objects, essential points and all points of PSh(R).','The finite-limit dictionary is valid with the adjoint correction C084. For an arbitrary limits-only doctrine, PSh(R) classifies flat functors, not all operation-preserving functors R→Set; essential points recover Kar(R), not R without idempotent completeness.','table proposed for arbitrary lambda; several cells and 2-fid queried','For the finite-product theory R=(finite free abelian groups)^op, operation-preserving Set models are all abelian groups. Flat functors are filtered colimits of its representable models, hence filtered colimits of finite free abelian groups and therefore torsion-free by Lazard. Z/2 is a model but not such a point. Separately SGAIV7.6(e) gives the necessary Karoubi completion for essential points.','SGA4IV7.6(e) and Stacks058C Theorem10.81.4 provide the exact two distinctions. Beke2004 Proposition0.1 and Corollary2.2 warn that equality of Set-model categories alone does not identify the original classifying topos.',['SGA4I1972','StacksFlatness','BekePresheafType'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-094')
addclaim(95,921,6,[92],'395–399','Paraphrase: a faithful theory morphism corresponds to a faithful inverse-image functor; the functor is overwritten from star-below to star-above.','Faithfulness of a structure-forgetting morphism on all internal model categories does not imply faithfulness of the induced classifying-topos inverse image. The table’s arrow direction and overwritten adjoint notation also require an ink check.','asserted dictionary row with a 2-fid question and overwritten star','Forgetting a group structure is faithful in every topos. The corresponding geometric morphism from the group classifier to the object classifier factors through the proper inhabited-object open subtopos, since every group has its unit. The generic object is not forced inhabited (the empty Set model witnesses propriety). Thus the inverse image is not conservative, hence not faithful: a faithful finite-limit/colimit-preserving functor between topoi reflects isomorphisms.','SGA4IV9.2 gives the open-subtopos interpretation and IV9.1.4 separates full faithfulness from geometric inclusion. The group/inhabitation counterexample is an independent reconstruction; it does not depend on the arbitrary-lambda failure in C094.',['SGA4I1972'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-095')

addproblem(47,'Essential points and presheaf reconstruction',[occurrence(6,[81],'40–45'),occurrence(6,[91,92],'341–405')],'Are topoi with enough essential points precisely presheaf topoi, and do their essential points recover the presenting category?','Yes to the first; the second recovers the Karoubi completion.','C086 and SGAIV7.6(a–g) give the exact criterion and its idempotent qualification.',['SGA4I1972'],worth='Commentary work only: the published exercise settles the criterion, and the necessary Karoubi completion should be shown explicitly in the dictionary.')
addproblem(48,'Local smallness of the category of all constructions',[occurrence(6,[82,85],'80–88;173','universal-reflection text canceled; size question live'),occurrence(7,[99],'130–163')],'Does the category of all constructions lie in the chosen universe, and does evaluation have the asserted universal property?','Currying proves the universal property if that category is a doctrine object and evaluation is exact; those are additional assumptions.','C087–088 separate formal two-Yoneda from the genuine size issue, using Vistoli3.6.2 and Kelly5.35.',['VistoliDescent','Kelly1982','DiLibertiOsmond2025'],worth='Worth a bounded task: choose one doctrine and compute the construction category from a small presentation before testing the proposed universal reflection.')
addproblem(49,'Bicolimits and the total cofibred category',[occurrence(6,[83,84],'121–166')],'Can a bicolimit be formed in Cat and then made exact by inverting comparison maps?','Yes when the relevant free envelope and admissible localization exist; invert cocartesian arrows for the covariant Grothendieck construction.','C089 matches Di Liberti–Osmond1.2.16 and6.3.2; the size of the comparison family remains an explicit premise.',['DiLibertiOsmond2025','StacksLocalization'],worth='Worth a concrete finite-doctrine reconstruction, principally to settle the variance and size of the comparison maps on these pages.')
addproblem(50,'Equality axioms versus invertibility axioms',[occurrence(6,[85],'183–190'),occurrence(7,[100],'223–229')],'Are equality axioms already included among axioms saying that arrows are invertible?','They are if the needed equalizers belong to the doctrine; otherwise a separate relation quotient is necessary.','C090 gives the exact equalizer argument and identifies the independent/fixed-basis coproduct distinction.',['Kelly1982','StacksLocalization'],worth='Commentary work only for equalizer doctrines; for a doctrine without equalizers, first write its explicit relation quotient instead of treating the question as unresolved.')
addproblem(51,'Stabilization from a small faithful basis',[occurrence(6,[88],'295–314'),occurrence(7,[102,103],'309–350')],'Does a small faithful basis force a small generated classifier and terminate the transfinite construction?','Faithfulness alone does not force operation generation; the bounded small theorem requires stronger data.','C091 and C101 give the exact obstruction and two finite witnesses; Kelly5.35 supplies the positive bounded replacement.',['Kelly1982','DiLibertiOsmond2025'],worth='Worth a bounded research task after replacing faithfulness by a precise generation condition; the present universal implication is already refuted.')
addproblem(52,'Conservativity, faithfulness and the canceled fullness claim',[occurrence(6,[88],'300–314','fully faithful struck out')],'What does a conservative exact functor force?','Preserving equalizers and reflecting isomorphisms imply faithfulness, not fullness.','C092 proves this and uses Groups→Set to separate the stronger canceled statement.',['RiehlContextOnline'],worth='Commentary work only: the retained claim has a short complete proof and the canceled strengthening has an elementary counterexample.')
addproblem(53,'Finite presentation and the envelope variance',[occurrence(6,[90],'316–338')],'Is the finitely presentable subcategory the finite-projective-limit envelope of the representables?','The relevant closure is by finite colimits and retracts; finite-limit closure fails over a noncoherent ring.','C093 supplies the square-zero-ring kernel counterexample and matches the finite-sketch theorem.',['LackTendas2023','LackPower2009'],worth='A reading check and concise commentary suffice: the mathematical variance is settled, while the ink must decide whether projective was actually written.')
addproblem(54,'The arbitrary-doctrine dictionary and its faithful row',[occurrence(6,[91,92],'341–405')],'Does the five-column dictionary hold for every lambda, including the faithful/2-faithful row?','Retain the finite-limit case, Karoubi completion, and the corrected adjoint condition; the arbitrary-lambda and faithful-inverse-image assertions fail.','C094–095 give independent torsion and inhabited-object counterexamples; SGAIV7.6 and9.2 identify the valid classifier distinctions.',['SGA4I1972','StacksFlatness','BekePresheafType'],worth='Worth a carefully bounded replacement table: state each column’s precise doctrine and variance, with these counterexamples as exclusion tests.')

